{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,320p' <original-home>/.codex/adversarial_review.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- P50 IMPORT USAGE ---' && P50Q=aristotle_responses/P50-quarantine/aristotle-stabilizer_two_lane_p50-n98s99xk_aristotle; rg -n 'FiniteCornerTrace\\\\.|KaplanskyDensity\\\\.' \\\"\"'$P50Q/AristotleCandidates/NormalFaithfulCornerTrace.lean\" && printf '\"'%s\\\\n' '--- SOURCE RANGES WITH NUMBERING ---' && nl -ba sources/manuscript/main.tex | sed -n '1280,1460p' | head -200\"","aggregated_output":"# Adversarial Review Protocol\n\nUse this before closing substantive Codex work. The goal is to find defects, not\nto summarize accomplishments.\n\n## Scope\n\n1. Identify the artifact types touched: `code`, `math`, `tex`, `computation`,\n   `source-backed`, `workflow`.\n2. Inspect the diff or new artifact against the user's request.\n3. Report findings by severity first. If there are no findings, say so and state\n   the remaining risk.\n\n## Universal Checks\n\n- Did the work answer the actual latest user request?\n- Did it modify unrelated files or broaden scope?\n- Did it overwrite, reformat, or revert pre-existing user changes?\n- Are generated files, caches, logs, plots, PDFs, or build artifacts handled\n  intentionally?\n- Are final claims stronger than the evidence supports?\n- Are verification commands actually relevant to the changed artifact?\n\n## Code Checks\n\n- Does the changed behavior match the stated interface and edge cases?\n- Are tests missing for a changed behavior, bug fix, parser, numerical routine,\n  CLI command, or user-facing workflow?\n- Are errors handled where inputs, files, network calls, or external tools can\n  fail?\n- Is there hidden global state, path sensitivity, nondeterminism, race risk, or\n  stale cache behavior?\n- Did dependency, config, or environment changes alter unrelated workflows?\n\n## Math Checks\n\n- Are all assumptions, domains, signs, conventions, normalizations, and boundary\n  conditions stated before use?\n- Are definitions used consistently throughout the derivation?\n- Is any implication actually reversible, or was a one-way statement used both\n  ways?\n- Are there division-by-zero, invertibility, regularity, convergence, branch-cut,\n  gauge, or boundary-term assumptions?\n- Do limiting cases, dimensions/units, symmetries, and known special cases agree?\n- Are symbolic substitutions justified rather than smuggling in the result?\n- Are claims labeled correctly as proof, derivation, check, conjecture, or\n  numerical evidence?\n\n## TeX And Source-Backed Checks\n\n- Does the document compile if compilation was expected?\n- Are labels, references, citations, bibliography entries, and figure paths valid?\n- Are source-backed claims tied to exact locators when required?\n- Is quoted or paraphrased material clearly separated from the agent's synthesis?\n- Does the exposition define terms before using them in load-bearing statements?\n\n## Computation Checks\n\n- Is the computation reproducible from a recorded command?\n- Are inputs, random seeds, versions, tolerances, and units documented when they\n  affect the result?\n- Are outputs interpreted correctly and not over-promoted beyond the computation?\n- Were independent sanity checks used where feasible: small cases, limiting\n  cases, dimensional analysis, symbolic identities, or alternate algorithms?\n\n## Closeout Template\n\nUse this structure internally, then surface the concise version to the user:\n\n```text\nFindings:\n- [severity] file/path:line - issue and consequence.\n\nChecks run:\n- command -> result.\n\nResidual risk:\n- what was not checked, or why the artifact is not a proof/complete validation.\n\nCleanup:\n- remaining dirty/untracked files and whether they are intentional.\n```\n--- P50 IMPORT USAGE ---\n213:    KaplanskyDensity.exists_net_compression_product d.isClosed_A\n--- SOURCE RANGES WITH NUMBERING ---\n  1280\t\\end{equation}\n  1281\tstrongly which proves that $q \\cU_\\G q$ is strongly dense in $q M_\\G q$. The\n  1282\tsame argument implies that for every $a,b \\in M_\\G$ there exist uniformly bounded nets $(a_i)_{i\\in I},(b_j)_{j\\in J}$ in $\\cU_\\G$ such that\n  1283\t\\begin{equation}\n  1284\t   q a_i q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q a q,\n  1285\t   \\qquad\n  1286\t   q b_j q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q b q.\n  1287\t\\end{equation}\n  1288\tTo argue for the convergence of the product, we can introduce a joint index $k$\\footnote{For example $k\n  1289\t    = (i,j)$ so that $a_{(i,j)} = a_i, b_{(i,j)} = b_j$ and $(i',j') \\preceq (i,j)\n  1290\t    \\Leftrightarrow i' \\preceq i \\land j' \\preceq j$},\n  1291\tso that by the same argument as in the proof of Lemma \\ref{lem:finite-trace}\n  1292\t\\begin{equation}\n  1293\t    q a_i q b_j q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q a q b q,\n  1294\t    \\qquad\n  1295\t    q b_j q a_i q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q b q a q.\n  1296\t\\end{equation}s\n  1297\tSince the nets $q a_n q, q\n  1298\tb_n q$ are uniformly bounded and the product on uniformly bounded sets is\n  1299\tstrongly continuous \\LS{REF},\n  1300\t\\begin{equation}\n  1301\t    \\begin{aligned}\n  1302\t        \\t_q( (q a q ) (q b q)) &= \\bra{\\W}  (q a q ) (q b q)\\ket{\\W} \\\\\n  1303\t                                & = \\lim_{k \\rightarrow \\infty}\\bra{\\W}  (q a_k\n  1304\t                                q ) (q b_k q)\\ket{\\W} \\\\\n  1305\t                                & = \\lim_{k \\rightarrow \\infty}\\bra{\\W}  (q b_k\n  1306\t                                q ) (q a_k q)\\ket{\\W} \\\\\n  1307\t                                &= \\bra{\\W}  (q b q ) (q a q)\\ket{\\W}\n  1308\t    \\end{aligned}\n  1309\t\\end{equation}\n  1310\twhere we used strong convergence of the nets in the second line which proves\n  1311\tcyclicity on $q M_\\G q$. Since $\\t_q$ is a restriction of a vector functional\n  1312\tto $qM_\\G q$ it is normal. Since on $q\\cH_\\w, q$ is the identity, we have that $\\t(1) =\n  1313\t\\bra{\\W} q \\ket{\\W} = \\langle \\W | \\W \\rangle = 1$ by Lemma \\ref{lem:q_exists}.\n  1314\tThis implies that $\\t_q$ defines a normal state on the von Neumann algebra $qM_\\G q$. \n  1315\t\\end{proof}\n  1316\t\n  1317\t\\subsection{Faithfulness and finiteness}\n  1318\tNow that we established that the state $\\t_q$ is tracial for $q M_\\G q$, we\n  1319\twant to use it to restrict the full algebra $M_\\G$. The central decomposition\n  1320\tof $M_\\G$ asserts that we can decompose our Hilbert space into an integral\n  1321\t\\begin{equation}\n  1322\t    \\cH_\\w \\simeq \\int_X \\cH_x d\\m(x),\n  1323\t\\end{equation}\n  1324\twhere $X$ is a measurable space equipped with a measure $\\m$, such that for\n  1325\teach measurable set $E \\subset X$ one obtains a projection $z_E$ which is in\n  1326\tthe center of $M_\\G$ such that\n  1327\t\\begin{equation}\n  1328\t    z_E \\cH_\\w \\simeq \\int_E \\cH_x d\\m(x).\n  1329\t\\end{equation}\n  1330\tSimilarly one has a decomposition \n  1331\t\\begin{equation}\n  1332\t    M_\\G \\simeq \\int_X M_x d\\m(x),\n  1333\t\\end{equation}\n  1334\twhere $M_x \\subset \\cB(\\cH_x)$ is a factor almost surely. Our goal is to proof\n  1335\tthat none of the $M_x$ can have type III. To do so, we will proof that every\n  1336\tone of the subalgebras appearing in the central decomposition has a finite\n  1337\tprojection, which arises from our $q$, which cannot happen for type III\n  1338\talgebras \\footnote{They are defined as not having finite projections}.\n  1339\tThe finiteness of a projection means that if there exist a partial isometry $v$\n  1340\twith\n  1341\t\\begin{equation}\n  1342\t    v^\\dagger v = q, \\quad v v^\\dagger \\leq q,\n  1343\t\\end{equation}\n  1344\tthen $v v^\\dagger = q$. A simple way to see finiteness is the existence of a\n  1345\tfaithful tracial state $\\t$, since then\n  1346\t\\begin{equation}\n  1347\t    \\t(q) = \\t(v v^\\dagger) = \\t(v^\\dagger v) \\Rightarrow \\t(q-v^\\dagger v) =\n  1348\t    0.\n  1349\t\\end{equation}\n  1350\tIf $\\t$ is faithful, i.e. for $x \\geq 0$, $\\t(x) = 0$ iff $x = 0$, this implies $q = v^\\dagger v$.\n  1351\tWe therefore need \n  1352\t\\begin{proposition}\\label{prop:faithful}\n  1353\t$\\tau_q$ is faithful on $q\\cM_\\Gamma q$.\n  1354\t\\end{proposition}\n  1355\t\\begin{proof}\n  1356\tLet $x\\in q\\cM_\\Gamma q$ satisfy\n  1357\t\\begin{equation}\n  1358\t  \\tau_q(x^\\dagger x)=0.\n  1359\t  \\label{eq:faithfulness-zero}\n  1360\t\\end{equation}\n  1361\tWe want to show that $x = 0$. We will do so, by demonstrating that $x \\ket{\\x}\n  1362\t= 0$ for a dense set of state $\\ket{\\x}$. This dense set \n  1363\twill be made of states of the form\n  1364\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:dense_states}\n  1365\t    \\ket{\\x_{a,b}} = \\pi_\\w(a) \\pi_\\w(b) \\ket{\\W},\n  1366\t\\end{equation}\n  1367\t with $a \\in \\cA_\\G, b \\in \\cA_{\\G^c}$, which form a norm-dense set of $\\cA$\n  1368\t and since we consider the GNS Hilbert space of $\\cA$ with respect to $\\w$, these states form a\n  1369\t dense set in $\\cH_\\w$.\n  1370\tFor $y\\in q\\cM_\\Gamma q$ we have \n  1371\t\\begin{align}\\label{eq:xyOmega_zero}\n  1372\t    \\norm{xy\\Omega}^2\n  1373\t  &=\\tau_q(y^\\dagger x^\\dagger xy)\\\\\n  1374\t  &=\\tau_q(x^\\dagger xy y^\\dagger)\\\\\n  1375\t  &=\\tau_q\\big((x^\\dagger x)^{1/2}yy^\\dagger(x^\\dagger x)^{1/2}\\big)\\\\\n  1376\t  &\\le \\|y\\|^2\\tau_q(x^*x)=0.\n  1377\t\\end{align}\n  1378\tIn the second line we used the definition of the state $\\t_q$, in the third we\n  1379\tused traciality of $\\t$ on $q M_\\G q$. In the fourth line we used that every\n  1380\tpositive operator has a square root together with traciality. In the fourth\n  1381\tline we used that \n  1382\t\\begin{equation}\n  1383\t    \\norm{y}^2 \\id \\geq y^\\dagger y,\n  1384\t\\end{equation}\n  1385\twhich follows from the definition of the operator norm. Conjugating the\n  1386\tpositive operator $ O_y = \\norm{y}^2 \\id - y^\\dagger y$ by $(x^\\dagger x)^{1/2}$ again leads to a\n  1387\tpositive operator. Since $\\t_q$ is the restriction of a vector functional, it\n  1388\tis positive, which applied to the positivity of\n  1389\t$x^\\dagger O_y x$ implies the last line.\n  1390\tWe thus have found that for every $y\\in q\\cM_\\Gamma q$\n  1391\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:all_xyOmega_zero}\n  1392\t  xy\\ket{\\W}=0.\n  1393\t\\end{equation}\n  1394\tNow for general $a\\in\\cA_\\Gamma$, we note that $x=xq$, since $x \\in q M_\\G q$\n  1395\tand $q\\ket{\\W}=\\ket{\\W}$ by Lemma \\ref{lem:q_exists}. This implies that\n  1396\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:x_a_zero}\n  1397\t  x\\pi_\\w(a)\\ket{\\W}\n  1398\t  =x q\\pi_\\w(a) q \\ket{\\W}=0.\n  1399\t\\end{equation}\n  1400\tSince operators in $\\cA_\\G$ commute with operators in $\\cA_{\\G^c}$ and $\\pi_\\w$\n  1401\tis a representation of $\\cA$, we have that\n  1402\t\\begin{equation}\n  1403\t  \\cM_\\Gamma\n  1404\t  =\\pi_\\omega(\\cA_\\Gamma)''\n  1405\t  \\subseteq\n  1406\t  \\pi_\\omega(\\cA_{\\Gamma^c})'.\n  1407\t  \\label{eq:M-commutes-complement}\n  1408\t\\end{equation}\n  1409\tSince $x \\in q M_\\G q \\subset M_\\G$, it commutes with\n  1410\t$\\pi_\\omega(\\cA_{\\Gamma^c})$.  For $a\\in\\cA_\\Gamma$ and $b\\in\\cA_{\\Gamma^c}$ we\n  1411\ttherefore have on the dense set of states from Eq. \\eqref{eq:dense_states}\n  1412\t\\begin{equation} \\label{eq:x_global_annihilation}\n  1413\t  x\\pi_\\omega(a)\\pi_\\omega(b)\\ket{\\W}\n  1414\t  =\\pi_\\omega(b)x\\pi_\\omega(a)\\ket{\\W} = 0,\n  1415\t\\end{equation}\n  1416\twhich implies $x = 0$ and thus proves faithfulness.\n  1417\t\\end{proof}\n  1418\tNote that we have only proved faithfulness of $\\t_q$ as a functional on $qM_\\G\n  1419\tq$, not of $\\w$ $M_\\G$. The discussion preceding Proposition\n  1420\t\\ref{prop:faithful} needed faithfulness on the algebra to proof finiteness of\n  1421\tthe projection. In the current situation we can still prove\n  1422\t\\begin{corollary}\\label{cor:q_finite}\n  1423\tThe projection $q$ is finite in $\\cM_\\Gamma$.\n  1424\t\\end{corollary}\n  1425\t\n  1426\t\\begin{proof}\n  1427\tAssume the existence of a partial isometry $v\\in\\cM_\\Gamma$ with\n  1428\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:v_leq_q}\n  1429\t  v^\\dagger v=q,\n  1430\t  \\qquad\n  1431\t  vv^\\dagger \\leq q.\n  1432\t\\end{equation}\n  1433\tSince $v$ is a partial isometry together with the inequality in \\eqref{eq:v_leq_q} implies that the rang\n  1434\tof $v$ is contained in the range of $q$, on which $q$ acts as the identity. \n  1435\tThis implies that $v = qv$. \n  1436\tBy the same logic, the support of $v$ is contained in the support of $q$, so\n  1437\tthat $v = vq$. Therefore $v = qvq$, which implies $v \\in q M_\\G q$.\n  1438\tNow we can apply the argument preceding Proposition \\ref{prop:faithful}.\n  1439\tTraciality of $\\t$ implies\n  1440\t\\begin{equation}\n  1441\t  \\tau_q(vv^*)=\\tau_q(v^*v)=\\tau_q(q)=1.\n  1442\t\\end{equation}\n  1443\tTherefore $\\tau_q(q-vv^*)=0$. Faithfulness of $\\t_q$ and $q-vv^*\\ge 0$ then\n  1444\timply $vv^*=q$.  Thus $q$ is finite.\n  1445\t\\end{proof}\n  1446\tThe above proof verbatim also proves\n  1447\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:finite_corner_finite_global}\n  1448\t    Let $M$ be a von Neumann algebra and $q \\in M$ a projection. Let $\\t_q$ be a\n  1449\t    faithful tracial functional on $q M q$. Then $q$ is finite with respect to $M$.\n  1450\t\\end{lemma}\n  1451\t\n  1452\t\\subsection{Fullness and exclusion of type III}\n  1453\tWe now can move on to prove the main theorem. In the end, we want to\n  1454\tproof that for every central projections $z \\in \\cZ(M_\\G)$, the algebra $zM_\\G$ contains a nonzero finite projection. The projection we pick will be $zq$\n  1455\tand we therefore first neew to show that, for every central projection $zq$ is\n  1456\tnonzero. This goes under the name of fullness.\n  1457\tThe central support $c_{M_\\G}(q)$ is the least central projection in\n  1458\t$\\cM_\\Gamma$ dominating $q$ \\cite[III.1.1.5]{Blackadar}.\n  1459\tThe projection $q$ is called \\emph{full} if its central support is the identity.\n  1460\tFor a full projection $q$, if one has any nonzero central projection $z$, then $zq$ is\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
